A colour palette manager publishes colour updates to many independent brush widgets. The manager keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:

```java
public void publish(Colour colour) {
    for (BrushListener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onColour(colour);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A configuration class in a painting application is written like this and is read from every brush handler:

```java
public class PaletteConfig {
    private static PaletteConfig instance;
    private PaletteConfig() { }
    public static PaletteConfig getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new PaletteConfig();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this code are justified?

A painting workflow generator fixes the order of the steps and lets subclasses vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class PaintWorkflow {
    public final Painting generate() {
        Palette palette = load();
        Palette prepared = transform(palette);
        return render(prepared);
    }
    protected Palette load() { return Palette.fromDefaultSource(); }
    protected abstract Palette transform(Palette palette);
    protected abstract Canvas render(Palette palette);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A painting tool sorts colour palettes with interchangeable algorithms:

```java
public interface SortStrategy { void performSort(List<Colour> input); }
public class BubbleSort implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class MergeSort  implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class Context { private List<Colour> colours; private SortStrategy sortAlgorithm; ... }
public class Policy  { private Context context; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this listing: the class that holds the colours and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `BubbleSort` and `MergeSort` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A reviewer rejects the following class:

```java
public class ColourService {
    private final SmtpMailer mailer = new SmtpMailer("mail.internal", 25);
    public void applyColour(Colour colour) { /* ... */ mailer.send(colour.getUserEmail(), "..."); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `ColourService` names a concrete low‑level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
